.TH MUX 1
.SH NAME
mux \- tiled and desktop terminal tabs with detachable sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mux
[COMMAND [ARG...]]
.br
.B mux new
NAME [COMMAND [ARG...]]
.br
.B mux attach
NAME
.br
.B mux list
.br
.B mux kill
NAME
.br
.B mux save
[NAME]
.br
.B mux restore
[NAME]
.br
.B mux saved
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run several terminal applications in one terminal. Each pane has a separate
shell, controlling terminal, job control and terminal screen. Tabs remember
their own tiled or floating layout. Without arguments, mux attaches live main,
otherwise restores the default saved workspace as main, or starts a fresh shell
if no default exists. mux new main explicitly starts fresh.
A command replaces the first pane's shell; later panes start the configured shell.
.PP
A detached session continues while the operating system runs. Close a GUI
terminal or disconnect from Telnet, then attach again from another terminal.
Live processes do not survive an operating-system shutdown. Saved layouts and
restart recipes can recreate a session after reboot. Only one terminal may
attach to a session at a time. mux kill also works on an attached session.
.PP
The top-right clock shows local time in 24-hour HH:MM format and updates while
the session is idle. It is hidden in terminals narrower than 20 columns to
leave room for tabs. Timezone rules come from the mux server's TZ environment,
or from /etc/TZ when TZ is unset. Changes to /etc/TZ appear on the next minute;
exporting TZ in a pane does not change its already-running parent mux server.
.SH DESKTOP TABS
Ctrl-B f creates a desktop tab, marked [D]. Drag title bars to move windows;
drag borders/corners to resize. Click a window or its bottom task-bar title to
focus and raise it. [_] minimizes, [+]/[-] maximizes/restores; [x] closes immediately.
The keyboard close command Ctrl-B x still asks for confirmation.
Minimized windows keep running and appear as dimmed task-bar buttons with stable
widths. Click a button or cycle with Ctrl-B Tab/o to restore it. When all windows
are minimized, ordinary input is not forwarded to hidden applications.
Side and bottom borders use the normal terminal background. Mux top/bottom bars
are dark gray; window headers are lighter gray with blue text for the active
window.
Mouse input in content belongs to the application. Shift-drag selects terminal
text for copying. Small terminals temporarily show only the focused pane;
preferred rectangles return when the terminal grows. Tiled tabs coexist in the
same session and share the pane limit. Ctrl-B w opens another desktop window.
.PP
Version 4 saves include rectangles, titles, stacking order, minimized state and
manual title/restart overrides. Versions 1 through 3 remain readable; old binaries
cannot read version 4 saves. Titles in older desktop saves remain manual names
until cleared.
.SH KEYS
Press Ctrl-B, release it, then the command key. Ordinary Tab, function keys,
Ctrl-C and Ctrl-Z are sent to the focused pane. Press Ctrl-B twice to send it
to the application. The status line shows the current pane number.
.TP
c
Create a tiled tab.
.TP
f
Create a floating desktop tab.
.TP
e
Open Run. Enter a program and arguments; Enter launches, Escape cancels, Ctrl-U
clears. Mouse buttons Run and Cancel are also available. Desktop tabs create a
new floating window; tiled tabs create a new tab. Commands inherit the selected
pane directory, run through the configured shell, and return to a shell on exit.
Unicode input and bracketed paste are supported; pasted newlines do not launch.
.TP
M
Minimize the focused desktop window, keeping its application running. Restore
through the task bar or Ctrl-B Tab/o. Minimized state survives workspace saves.
.TP
w
Open a new window in the desktop tab.
.TP
m, v
Move or resize the desktop window with arrows or h/j/k/l. Enter, Escape or
Ctrl-C finishes the mode.
.TP
t
Set a manual desktop window title. Normally titles follow foreground programs
launched by the system shell and return to shell at the next idle prompt.
Ctrl-U and Enter clear the manual name and resume automatic naming.
.TP
n, p, 1 through 8
Next, previous, or numbered tab.
.TP
,
Rename tab. Ctrl-U clears the name; Enter accepts; Escape cancels.
.TP
%
Split the pane into left and right panes.
.TP
\(dq
Split the pane into top and bottom panes.
.TP
Tab, o
Focus the next pane in this tab.
.TP
h, j, k, l, arrow keys
Focus left, down, up, or right.
.TP
+, -
Grow or shrink the focused split, or both dimensions of a desktop window.
.TP
z
Zoom the focused pane to fill the tab, or restore the split layout.
.TP
!
Move the focused pane into a new tab, preserving its running process, PTY,
queued input, screen and scrollback. The original split closes around its
sibling. A pane already occupying its own tab is left alone.
.TP
S
Save this session's current layout and restart recipes to disk.
.TP
C
Edit the focused pane's restart command. Empty means an interactive shell.
This changes what restore launches; it does not affect the currently running
application. Ctrl-U clears the field, Enter accepts, Escape cancels.
.TP
A
Clear both restart overrides and use automatic foreground command/directory
capture for the focused pane again.
.TP
D
Edit the focused pane's restart directory. It must be an existing absolute
guest path. This changes the saved startup directory without changing the
current application's working directory.
.TP
[
Enter scrollback. Use j/k or arrows, b/f or PageUp/PageDown, g/G for top/bottom.
Space starts or cancels line selection. y copies selected lines, or the current
line. / searches backward, wrapping at the oldest line; n repeats. q or Escape
returns to the application. Copying also leaves scrollback.
.TP
]
Paste copied text into the focused pane. Clipboard is internal to this session.
.TP
x
Close the pane and all processes in its session, after y/n confirmation.
Unsaved application work is discarded. Exiting the pane shell also closes it.
.TP
d
Detach; applications continue running.
.TP
?, r
Show keyboard help, or redraw the terminal.
.SH SAVING AND RESTORING
Ctrl-B then S saves the current session and updates $HOME/.mux/default.
Alternatively, run mux save NAME from a pane, another terminal or a script;
it works while attached or detached. NAME defaults to main. mux saved lists
saved sessions. The default is an independent atomic copy of the last save.
.PP
After restarting the operating system, plain mux restores this default as main.
A live main session is attached first. mux new NAME and mux COMMAND bypass
implicit restore. mux restore NAME restores an explicitly named save. Restore
recreates tiled/desktop layouts, titles, stacking, focus, zoom and configuration
using new processes. An invalid default is reported; mux new main starts fresh.
.PP
The interactive system shell reports foreground external commands and their
arguments and launch directories to mux. Idle shells report their current
directory. At save, the reported group must still own the foreground terminal.
Arguments are safely quoted; automatically restored apps return to a shell on
exit. Completed commands and background launches are not replayed. Pipelines,
redirected output, custom shells and jobs resumed with fg may need an explicit
recipe. Without a valid report mux retains the pane's startup recipe.
.PP
Prefix C and D override automatic command/directory capture. Empty C means an
interactive shell. Prefix A clears both overrides. Overrides survive restore;
old version-1/2 snapshots retain their original recipes as explicit overrides.
A command supplied to mux new NAME COMMAND is recorded with its arguments.
.PP
Saving does not checkpoint process memory, shell variables, background jobs,
unsaved editor buffers, terminal contents or scrollback text. Applications
recover their own saved data. Saving is explicit; shutdown does not replace a
previous snapshot automatically. Remove $HOME/.mux/default to disable automatic
restore. mux kill stops a live session and keeps its
saved layout. Remove $HOME/.mux/NAME to delete that saved layout.
.PP
Snapshots are versioned text files in a mode-0700 $HOME/.mux directory, written
atomically with mode 0600. The complete graph and all restart directories are
validated before any pane is started. Invalid, truncated, foreign-owned or
publicly accessible snapshots are rejected. Startup failure cleans up panes
already created and leaves the saved file intact. Commands are limited to
1023 octets and directory paths to 255 octets.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Read $HOME/.muxrc when starting a session. Changes apply to new sessions.
Lines beginning with # and unknown options are ignored.
.PP
history=200
.br
prefix=b
.br
shell=/bin/sh
.PP
history sets retained lines per pane, 0 through 1000. prefix is a lowercase
letter whose control character becomes the command prefix. shell is an absolute
executable path, without command-line arguments.
.SH LIMITS
Eight tabs and twelve panes per session. Both kernels provide sixteen PTYs and
thirty-two descriptors per process; GUI terminals, Telnet and other sessions
share the PTY pool. Process and memory limits can prevent creation earlier.
Screens support up to 240 columns and 100 outer rows. If splits no longer fit,
only the focused pane is displayed until the terminal becomes large enough.
.PP
Scrollback has a bounded internal text clipboard (8191 characters), ASCII search
and line selection. Screen resize preserves top-left content without text
reflow. Terminal colours use a sixteen-colour palette; extended colours are
approximated. Unicode cells and mouse interaction are supported; shared simultaneous
attachment is not implemented. History and copied text remain in guest memory.
.SH FILES
$HOME/.muxrc
.br
$HOME/.mux/NAME (named recipes), $HOME/.mux/default (last saved workspace)
.br
/tmp/mux-UID/NAME
.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), ptysh(1), stty(1)
